# Which lane owns which bench (T100 D10, Alex 2026-10-25: every bench belongs to exactly one lane). One line per bench of ci/sims.txt:
#   <bench>: host | core:<id> (a DFX core) | large:<id> (a large core) | none
# host = the host lane (host/, the reference core, the host app, cores/common, the TRX modules of cores/common); core:/large: = the core whose build the
# bench proves; none = a source tree that is never built (cores/c64u, T115), its benches are never planned. A bench is read from the code it runs,
# not from its name: amiga_cfg, amiga_cpu020, amiga_fastpat, amiga_fbwin and amiga_z3 run the A1200 build (TG68K, AGA) and belong to amiga1200; the controls
# that run the other core's file as a negative control do not make the bench theirs. ci/orchestrate.py check: a row for every bench, a lane of this
# tree, and the bench's ci/sims.deps line names that core's directory (host: host/). ci/sims.sh makes a failed bench soft only when its owner is
# an experimental core; ci/plan.py runs a bench when its owner lane fires (or its own bench source changed).

